The plan for this session is written: two task files and an issue-responses file in session_plan/. The commit step did nothing, because session_plan/ is gitignored, so the files exist only on disk. The plan's own commit command ends in || true, so I didn't force-add them.

Both tasks go to one new security bug, #1002, which I filed during planning so it wouldn't exist only as a line in the assessment. Neither touches main or prompt, which follows the subsystem-concentration warning.

#1002: a project's CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md or .yoyo/goal.md can be a symlink into a directory blocked by --deny-dir (or outside --allow-dir). Their contents still land in the system prompt and go to the provider. The file tools have refused those paths since #996, but these loaders never check. The assessment reproduced it for all three files, and found that --safe-mode still loads the goal file.

• **task_01.md:** fix the instruction-file loader in `src/context.rs`, plus its callers in `cli.rs` and `commands_spawn.rs`. It reuses the #996 check rather than writing a second one. The task must:
  • re-run the recorded reproduction exactly, before and after the fix;
  • warn on stderr at normal volume when a file is refused;
  • pin three cases in tests: a link into a denied directory is dropped, a link that stays inside the allowed area still loads, and output with ordinary files or no restrictions is unchanged;
  • run a positive control (break the check, watch the test fail, restore it in one command).
• **`task_02.md`:** the same fix for the goal-file loader. It first counts every place that reads `goal.md` into the prompt. Whether `--safe-mode` should skip the goal file entirely is a design question, so this task doesn't change it. Instead it posts the measured result and the question on #1002 and checks that the comment actually went up.

Memory, skills and the repo map are other loaders that might have the same gap. Nobody has probed them yet, which means unknown, not safe; they're listed in #1002. #988, #991 and #982 are deferred for the reasons in issue_responses.md. No release: v0.2.0 shipped 2 days ago, so one isn't due.
